# Wheel of Judgment

> GM-facing narrative for the adventure *Wheel of Judgment: An Adventure in the Underworld*
> (L5R13DLC01a, Fantasy Flight Games). Boxed **read-aloud** text is reproduced verbatim as
> blockquotes; intervening GM procedure (check TNs, skirmish bookkeeping, branch handling) is
> summarized faithfully — the full detail lives in the source PDF. The nine statblocks are in
> `l5r5e-0.4-wheel-of-judgment-cast.ttrpg`, and the three-part structure in
> `l5r5e-0.4-wheel-of-judgment.arc`. The adventure uses the players' own deceased characters — there
> are no pregens — and in Part Two they are temporarily reincarnated as oni. The free supplement
> *Deathly Turns* adds further underworld encounters.

*Wheel of Judgment* is played by characters who have died in the Mortal Realm. It takes place in the
underworld, where everyone and everything must eventually pass through the Realm of Waiting. PCs may
witness disturbing sights in the Realm of Evil; the GM can tone the horror up or down to taste. The
central antagonist, **the Phantasm** (the Phantasmal Oni), is a demon of illusion charged — or so it
claims — by Fu Leng himself to sabotage the running of Meido and steal worthy souls for Jigoku's
growing demonic army. The less the players know going in, the better.

---

## Part One: Meido, the Realm of Waiting

The PCs awaken in Meido after dying in another adventure or at the start of the session:

> A vast plain stretches away on all sides, one great field of whispering grasses, dotted with distant
> figures. People, horses, wolves, and even what appear to be goblins, all walk toward the horizon,
> where the towers of a palace rise into the white sky. You feel a compulsion to join them.

The PCs are dressed and equipped as at death (a body killed in battle bears its fatal wound, without
pain); their stats are unchanged. The many-tiered palace of **Emma-Ō** is visible from everywhere,
surrounded by eight citadels and, seen from above, resembling an enormous **wheel** with the palace at
the center and the queues of souls as its spokes. The mazoku work incessantly in the offices and
scriptoriums lining the avenues.

**The Guard.** The first mazoku the PCs meet is **Minroki** (use the **Mazoku Guard** profile), a
firm-but-fair guard who herds new arrivals into the queue and reports bribe attempts to his partner
**Rikakima**. They soon learn the records of their lives are missing, and they cannot be judged until
those are found.

**The Queue.** Joining a queue, the PCs may speak to other spirits — many distant and uninterested,
having gained the **Deadened** condition from Meido's supernatural atmosphere (which the mazoku collect
as dust to keep the dead in line):

> The queue stretches away ahead of you, as far as you can see. Samurai, peasants, and even the huge
> form of a troll stand in an orderly line, gazing silently ahead. They're not moving. How long have
> they been standing there? Birds perch silently on the shoulders of some. Before you reach the end of
> the queue, a man in the clothes of a traveling merchant comes from further ahead and takes his place
> behind a fidgeting courtier.

Among the queued are a Deadened **Forest Troll** (core p.321) impaled on a spear, and the distressed
courtier **Reju Tabito** (Seasoned Courtier, core p.313), who fears he was replaced and murdered so a
spirit or shinobi could get close to his young student, a Seppun boy called Akio.

**Exploring Meido.** The PCs may visit the citadels — small buildings that are supernaturally vast
within. **Meido Dust** deadens most shades: the shimmering dust works only on the shades of the dead
(not oni), and a shade lingering in Meido makes a **TN 1 Meditation check** to resist the atmosphere,
the TN rising by 1 each repeat. In **Office 801**, a mazoku magistrate denies remembering the PCs'
papers, but a bribe or a **TN 3 Social check (Void 1, Earth 4)** wins the admission that he sent them
on to Rikakima. **Rikakima, Mazoku Magistrate** — Minroki's glum, bribe-taking partner — is the thread
that leads, through the evidence of sabotage, to the office of the maō **Burakakin**, who offers the
PCs a deal: uncover who is sabotaging Meido from within Jigoku, and he will restore their chance at
judgment.

---

## Part Two: Jigoku, the Realm of Evil

Burakakin reincarnates the PCs as **oni** — they must leave everything behind, keeping anything they
accumulate in Jigoku, though items found there are often Unholy. Over a millennium ago Fu Leng crashed
into Jigoku, and its oni — beings who once existed to suffer for their prior sins — cast off their
shackles to spread evil and Taint. **Mahō** is usable in Jigoku (kansen flit about the caverns), and
any PC who uses it has it counted in their final judgment.

**The Guide.** Leaving the cavern where they arrive, the PCs meet the **Trickster Oni**, who calls
herself **Torikkusutā** — a purple, mazoku-like oni in rags who claims she was sent to uncover the
source of sabotage but was separated from her partner. It is all a lie; she can sense the PCs' mission
and offers to guide them (until betrayal seems useful), and suggests looking in the archives of the
**Fortress of Fu Leng**. In truth she was wrongly sent to Jigoku — in life a Togashi monk who should
have returned to that order.

**Exploring Jigoku.** The Realm of Evil is a shifting maze of caverns and tunnels. Notable places
include the **River of Blood** — flowing with the ichor that serves as blood in oni forms, from which
any oni (the PCs included) may drink once per day to heal all fatigue and remove Bleeding, Dying, and
Wounded — and the **Gaping Maw**, a long tunnel corresponding to the Festering Pit in Ningen-dō, a
possible but arduous route out toward the Shadowlands.

**Rooting Out Corruption.** To fulfill the mazoku's task, the PCs must uncover the identities of the
compromised mazoku. Searching Fu Leng's fortress, they can recover — in **The Archive** — a scroll
that looks like a list: in fact a coded roster of mazoku working for or compromised by the Phantasm,
unreadable without the cipher found on the skin of an oni in the **Repository of All Knowledge**.
Among the fortress's oni are the honest-dueling **Bladed Oni** (in life a samurai), the knowledge-mad
**Word-Hungry Oni** and its detachable **Word-Hungry Hands** (in life a Kuni shugenja), and the
purposeless **Scribes of Jigoku** — one of them, in the Archive, an agent of the Phantasm. The Archive
holds the PCs' own copied papers and forgeries turning their past deeds into crimes; a **cloud of
white mist** watching there is the **Phantasmal Oni** itself, spying invisibly.

The Bladed Oni, the guardian of the Gaping Maw, and the Phantasm all claim to serve Fu Leng and are
wary of each other; the PCs can turn them against one another, and learn from the Word-Hungry Oni (an
agent of the Phantasm) or its enemies exactly where the list and cipher are.

---

## Part Three: Returning

The PCs journey home through **Gaki-dō, the Realm of the Hungry Dead**, their demonic forms now making
them natural enemies of the mazoku guards. The tunnel into Gaki-dō is too narrow for larger oni:

> After the darkness of Jigoku, the lightless tunnel is strangely familiar. It stretches on without
> turning or branching, and it seems you walk for days until a spot of weak grey light appears ahead.
> Finally, you emerge amid decaying ruins of crumbling stone and splitting wood under an oppressively
> grey sky. The deathly urban sprawl stops short of the bank of a sluggish, green-grey river that oozes
> through the shadows.

**The Gaki** — starving spirits of those who indulged their appetites in life — mostly ignore the PCs,
but some attack ravenously; the TN of **invocation checks made in Gaki-dō increases by 1**. Following
the direction of the lost Tenth Kami **Ryoshun**, the PCs may cross with minimal interaction and pass
into Meido. Aware of their meddling, the **Phantasm** follows them in (*Phantasmal Interference*),
conjuring illusions of food and obstacles along the way.

**Enter the Phantasm.** As the PCs reach Burakakin to reveal the compromised mazoku, the Phantasmal
Oni manifests and calls its allies to action:

> A shrill, insect-like buzzing builds to a painful crescendo as a misty form draws into itself,
> becoming a bulging white mass three stories tall that writhes and contracts and finally solidifies.
> Its new form has the appearance of marble: sickly pale with green veins, its slick surface reflecting
> the light and dazzling its enemies. Hideous horns sprout from its head, and its mouth moves
> ceaselessly, whispering commands to its allies.

**Battle Between the Realms.** The hidden oni of Meido reveal themselves and attack the mazoku (use the
Sinister Oni profile, core p.320). An oni attacking a shade (or vice versa) increases the check TN by
2 — but not the mazoku, who interact freely with both. The PCs may join in (spending Opportunity on
initiative for a free Attack against any oni but the Phantasm), or try to keep clear — but the
Phantasm has a personal vendetta against the thieves of its list and hunts them with **Powerful
Illusions**. The final battle can be run as a skirmish; if the Phantasm falls, the rest of the oni
scatter and the mazoku finish them.

**Is This the End?** As the Phantasmal Oni dies, it attempts one last illusion of victory:

> Just when it seems the great oni is weakening, its body emits a sudden light, and in the blinding
> flash, three duplicates of the massive demon appear, crushing the offices and gates of Meido with
> their fists and trampling mazoku beneath their huge stone feet. The ground shakes beneath them and
> spirits scatter and flee in all directions. The realm is surely lost.

The PCs may see through the illusion (the TN raised by 1); either way, the oni dies and the illusion
fades:

> Broken buildings are restored, fallen mazoku back on their feet. The massive oni lies with its stone
> arms shattered, its mouth silenced. The realm is saved.

Should the PCs fail entirely, **Emma-Ō** himself arrives to destroy the oni — victorious but at great
cost — and Burakakin and Emma-Ō still honor their promises, grateful for the exposed plot.

**Final Judgment.** Burakakin gathers the PCs and transports them to the **Palace of Emma-Ō**. If they
are still in oni form, he slits their throats so their shades slip free once more, and they enter:

> The court is vast, and light shines in through an open ceiling. Mazoku kneel to either side of a
> dais, which bears a throne of polished glass. Seated there, a divinely imposing and regal figure,
> clad in ornate robes of office, regards you thoughtfully. Burakakin tells you, "Bow before the Great
> Emma-Ō, Judge of the Dead!"

Burakakin summarizes the PCs' deeds — sins committed, desires and fears resisted — and lets them speak
their own case and make requests (to return to Ningen-dō; to keep items they found; and so on). Emma-Ō
then delivers his judgment:

> The Divine Emma-Ō speaks in a deep and powerful voice that causes the great chamber to tremble. He
> explains that no one can violate the cycle of life and death. Yet, he needs time to consider your
> most unusual case. He suggests that you enjoy his garden while you wait, particularly the very fine
> view from atop the hill.

**Return.** Ushered into a manicured garden with a small, plain hill at its center, the PCs climb the
hill — and find themselves returned to Ningen-dō, the chance to resume the lives they lost earned at
last. How they conducted themselves throughout the underworld shapes the details of that return.
